WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.000
Impossible burgers have been criticized
for containing heme iron.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.960
What does that mean
for consumers’ health?

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.489
Check out the video.

00:00:08.489 --> 00:00:15.000
"Heme Iron in Impossible Burgers and Cancer Risk"

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.520
In an editorial in the Journal of
the American Medical Association,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.600
the chair of nutrition at
Harvard pointed out that many

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.280
plant-based meat alternatives,
such as Beyond Meat and

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.960
the Impossible Burger,
can be high in sodium.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.560
But an issue specific to the Impossible
Burger was the heme they add, derived

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.640
from soybean plants to enhance the
product's meaty flavor and appearance.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.680
Safety analyses have failed to find any
toxicity risk specific to the soy heme

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.240
they have yeast churn out.
The FDA has agreed both

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.200
for use as a flavor and
color enhancer: safe.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.600
In other words, just as safe as the heme
found in blood and muscle in meat.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.880
But how much is that really saying?

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.440
The concern raised in the op-ed, for
example, was that higher intake of heme

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.120
has been associated with an elevated
risk of developing type 2 diabetes.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:11.000
But not just diabetes, killer #7 in
the United States, higher dietary

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.440
intake of heme iron
is associated

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.560
with an increased risk of
cardiovascular disease as well,

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:21.840
killers #1, 4 and 13: heart disease,
stroke, and high blood pressure.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.080
But since heme is found
mostly in meat,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.960
heme intake may just
be a marker for meat intake.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.200
It's like with diabetes: three
meta-analyses published to date,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.040
and they all reported the same link.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.920
But there's lots of reasons why
meat may increase diabetes risk,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.280
like advanced glycation end products,
produced when animal products

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.280
are baked, broiled, grilled,
fried, or barbequed.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.280
So how do we know that the heme
isn't just some innocent bystander?

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.240
The same issue arises about
the link between heme intake

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.160
and increased breast cancer risk.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:59.920
Since heme iron is coming from
animal foods, it could be any

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.440
of the other meat components,
like animal fat or meat mutagens,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.320
compounds in meat that
can cause DNA mutations.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.680
And hey, what about all the hormonal
steroids implanted into cattle

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.200
that may play a role in the
development of breast cancer?

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.280
A study in Japan found that
beef imported from the United States

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:23.040
contained up to 600 times the
levels of estrogens like estradiol.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.920
US beef.
Japanese beef.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.200
And higher consumption of estrogen-rich
beef due to hormone implantation

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.640
may facilitate estrogen
accumulation in the human body

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.480
and thus affecting women's
risk for breast cancer.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.240
So yeah, heme iron intake was
associated with breast cancer risk,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.760
but maybe that's just because the heme
and the hormones are traveling together

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.320
in the same package: meat.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.280
This is about as good as any
observational study can do.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.280
The NIH-AARP study is the largest
prospective study on diet and health

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.800
ever, following more than a half million
men and women for over a decade now.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.680
With such a huge dataset they
could take advantage of the fact that

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.920
different meats have different amounts
of heme, so they could try to tease out

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.920
the heme components by, in effect,
comparing people eating different

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.280
amounts of heme, but the
same amount of meat

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.920
to see if heme is independently
associated with disease.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.200
And indeed, that's what they showed:
an independent association not only

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.360
from nitrites in processed meat, but heme
and mortality from almost all causes:

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.720
death from diabetes, heart disease,
stroke, respiratory disease,

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.600
kidney disease, liver disease,
cancer, and all causes put together.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.520
They calculated that about
one-fifth of the association between

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.760
like eating burgers and the
shortening of your lifespan

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.920
could be statistically accounted
for by just the heme itself,

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.200
but that's assuming cause-and-effect.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.760
Even an independent association
is still an association. You can't

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.480
prove cause and effect until you put it
to the test in interventional studies.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:10.160
Normally, we don't necessarily
care about the mechanism.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.400
When the World Health Organization
designated bacon, ham, hot dogs,

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.240
luncheon meat, sausage
to be Group 1 carcinogens,

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.360
meaning we know these products
cause cancer in human beings,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.640
who cares if it's the heme iron, or
the heterocyclic aromatic amines,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.120
or the polycyclic aromatic
hydrocarbons, or the N-nitrosamines.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.440
They're all wrapped up in the
same place, processed meat,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.560
which we know causes cancer.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.080
So we should just try to stay away
from it, regardless of the mechanism.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.640
But with the advent of the Impossible
Burger, we really do have to know,

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:48.160
because for the first time we have
lots of heme without any actual meat.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.560
So we need to know if
the heme itself is harmful.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:55.360
For that, we'll have to turn
to interventional studies,

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.560
which we'll cover next.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.320
In muscle meat there’s a
heme protein that contributes

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.400
to the meaty taste of meat.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:10.000
Well, there’s a heme protein in
the roots of soybean plants too

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.600
that can be churned out to
provide a similar flavor and aroma

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.280
in plant-based meat,
which is used to make

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.320
the Impossible Burger possible.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.400
The question is: are
there any downsides?

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.080
When the European Food Safety
Authority was considering

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.800
the safety of adding heme iron
to foods, their main concern

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.760
was a potential increased
risk of colon cancer.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.840
We know meat causes cancer.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.520
Processed meat—bacon, ham, hot dogs,
lunch meat, and sausage—

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.760
is considered a Group 1
carcinogen, meaning we know

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.320
it causes cancer in people,
with the same level of certainty

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.640
that something like
smoking causes cancer.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.040
Whereas something like
a burger, just probably

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.160
causes cancer in
people, kind of like DDT.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.200
But what’s the role of heme iron?

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.240
I mean, there are all
sorts of potential mechanisms

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:00.680
to explain the cancer risk.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.840
Meat’s got the pro-inflammatory
long-chain omega-6

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.320
arachidonic acid, more of the
aging- and cancer-associated

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.800
methionine, trans fat,
endogenous hormones like IGF-1,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.040
not to mention the ones
that are implanted in animals

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.920
as hormonal growth-promoters.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.400
Then there are all the
toxic pollutants that build up

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.200
the food chain, like pesticides—

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.320
I didn’t even know
about the formaldehyde.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.600
According to the prestigious IARC,

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.920
the International Agency
for Research on Cancer,

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.760
there is strong evidence that
heterocyclic aromatic amines

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.080
contribute to the
cancer-causing mechanism.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.920
These DNA-damaging compounds
are formed when muscle tissue

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.960
is exposed to high, dry heat –
grilling, roasting, baking, broiling_

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.160
basically anything above
steaming or stewing.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:52.280
There is also strong evidence
that the formation of so-called

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.920
N-nitroso compounds contribute
to the cancer-causing mechanism.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.720
These are carcinogens that
can form inside our gut

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:03.480
when we eat the meat.
But there is also strong evidence,

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.480
according to the IARC,
that heme iron contributes

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.480
to the cancer-causing mechanism.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.680
Normally, I might leave it there,
but other authoritative bodies

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.400
I respect, like the American
Institute for Cancer Research

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.960
and the World Cancer Research
Fund, are more tentative.

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.440
While they agree there is some
evidence that the consumption

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.160
of foods containing heme iron
might increase the risk

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.440
of colorectal cancer,
they consider the evidence

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.120
suggesting such a
connection to be limited.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.960
Much of the available evidence is
based on lab animal data

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.160
such as this: in which
dietary heme was found

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.160
to disrupt the gut flora,
aggravate inflammation,

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.480
and potentiate the development
of intestinal tumors in mice.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:48.640
But it’s critical to note
that in all the laboratory

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:50.360
animal models that
have been used,

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:53.480
the rodents ingested
meat or heme equivalent

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:57.800
to people eating up to
40,000 pounds of meat a day.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.000
Even the smallest
dose would be like

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.680
a dozen Impossible Burgers a day.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.360
It’s easy to see how casual
readers could get confused, though.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.960
In this study, ascribing a
central role for heme iron

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.280
in the colon cancer
development associated with meat,

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:18.160
the authors claimed they
were aimed at determining

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.080
at nutritional doses, which
was the main factor involved

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.120
in cancer promotion. So,
doses of heme were chosen

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.480
to mimic red meat
consumption and… Boom!

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.080
Significant increase in tumor load.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.920
The researchers conclude
that their findings

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:36.200
strongly “suggest that at
concentrations that are in line

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.560
with human meat consumption,
heme iron is associated

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:42.920
with the promotion of
colon [cancer development].”

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:46.200
But if you look at the
actual diet they were given

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:49.800
and do the math, that’s
500 times the level of heme

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.400
found in people’s diets, in excess of

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.960
like 70 pounds of meat a day.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:58.000
Of course, even if they
really did use the right doses

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.840
they’re still going to end up
with data on the wrong species,

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:26.400
which brings us to clinical
studies which we’ll explore next.

